{"id":10910,"date":"2024-07-20T12:00:19","date_gmt":"2024-07-20T02:00:19","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/coastaldemolitions.com\/?p=10910"},"modified":"2024-08-26T11:45:09","modified_gmt":"2024-08-26T01:45:09","slug":"asbestos-in-your-home-identifying-and-dealing-with-this-hazardous-material","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/coastaldemolitions.com\/asbestos-in-your-home-identifying-and-dealing-with-this-hazardous-material\/","title":{"rendered":"Asbestos in Your Home: Identifying and Dealing with this Hazardous Material"},"content":{"rendered":"<h2>What is Asbestos?<\/h2>\n<p>Asbestos is a naturally occurring mineral that was widely used in building materials due to its durability and heat-resistant properties. Commonly found in older homes, asbestos can be present in insulation, roofing, flooring, and various other construction materials. While it was once hailed for its versatility, asbestos is now known to pose serious health risks.<\/p>\n<h2>Health Risks of Asbestos<\/h2>\n<p>Exposure to asbestos fibres can lead to severe health issues, including asbestosis, lung cancer, and mesothelioma. These diseases often develop years after exposure, making early identification and removal of asbestos in your home crucial for long-term health.<\/p>\n<h2>Identifying Asbestos in Your Home<\/h2>\n<p>Determining whether your home contains asbestos can be challenging, especially since asbestos fibres are microscopic. However, there are certain materials and areas where asbestos is commonly found:<\/p>\n<h3>Insulation<\/h3>\n<p>Asbestos was widely used in insulation materials, particularly in homes built before the 1980s. Check for insulation in attics, walls, and around pipes and boilers.<\/p>\n<h3>Roofing and Siding<\/h3>\n<p>Asbestos can be found in roof shingles and siding materials. If your home has old roofing or siding, there&#8217;s a chance it contains asbestos.<\/p>\n<h3>Flooring<\/h3>\n<p>Vinyl floor tiles and the adhesive used to install them may contain asbestos. These were common in homes built before the 1980s.<\/p>\n<h3>Textured Paint and Patching Compounds<\/h3>\n<p>Decorative textures applied to ceilings and walls, as well as patching compounds used before the late 1970s, may contain asbestos.<\/p>\n<h2>What to Do if You Suspect Asbestos<\/h2>\n<p>If you suspect that materials in your home contain asbestos, it&#8217;s crucial not to disturb them. Asbestos fibres can become airborne when materials are damaged or disturbed, increasing the risk of inhalation. Instead, follow these steps:<\/p>\n<h3>Hire a Professional Inspector<\/h3>\n<p>Contact a licensed asbestos inspector to conduct a thorough examination of your home. They can take samples of suspected materials and have them tested in a laboratory.<\/p>\n<h3>Avoid DIY Removal<\/h3>\n<p>Asbestos removal should only be performed by trained and certified professionals. DIY removal can release dangerous fibres into the air, posing a risk to you and your family.<\/p>\n<h3>Containment and Removal<\/h3>\n<p>If asbestos is found, a professional removal service will safely contain and remove the hazardous material. This process involves sealing off the area, using specialised equipment, and following strict safety protocols to prevent contamination.<\/p>\n<h2>Why Choose Professional Asbestos Removal?<\/h2>\n<p>Professional asbestos removal ensures that the hazardous material is handled safely and in compliance with regulations. Certified removal experts have the necessary training, equipment, and experience to manage asbestos risks effectively. They also follow disposal procedures to prevent environmental contamination.<br \/>\nIdentifying and dealing with asbestos in your home is essential for maintaining a safe living environment. By understanding where asbestos may be found and taking appropriate actions, you can protect your health and that of your family. Check out our asbestos removal services to learn more about how we can assist you with safe and efficient asbestos management.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>What is Asbestos? Asbestos is a naturally occurring mineral that was widely used in building materials due to its durability and heat-resistant properties. Commonly found in older homes, asbestos can be present in insulation, roofing, flooring, and various other construction materials. While it was once hailed for its versatility, asbestos is now known to pose [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":13,"featured_media":10632,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"_acf_changed":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[1],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-10910","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-tips-advice"],"acf":[],"yoast_head":"<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO plugin v28.0 - https:\/\/yoast.com\/product\/yoast-seo-wordpress\/ -->\n<title>Asbestos in Your Home: Identifying and Dealing with this Hazardous Material - Coastal Demolitions &amp; Asbestos Removal<\/title>\n<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index, follow, max-snippet:-1, max-image-preview:large, max-video-preview:-1\" \/>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https:\/\/coastaldemolitions.com\/asbestos-in-your-home-identifying-and-dealing-with-this-hazardous-material\/\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:locale\" content=\"en_US\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Asbestos in Your Home: Identifying and Dealing with this Hazardous Material - Coastal Demolitions &amp; Asbestos Removal\" \/>\n<meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"What is Asbestos? Asbestos is a naturally occurring mineral that was widely used in building materials due to its durability and heat-resistant properties. Commonly found in older homes, asbestos can be present in insulation, roofing, flooring, and various other construction materials. While it was once hailed for its versa